Note that $u^2 \le u^4 = u^2 \cdot u^2 \le 1.$  Observe that $\sin^5 x + \sin^4 x \cos x + \sin^3 x \cos^2 x + \sin^2 x \cos^3 x + \sin x \cos^4 x = \sin x (\sin^4 x + \sin^3 x \cos x + \sin^2 x \cos^2 x + \sin x \cos^3 + \cos^4 x).$  Hence,
\begin{align*}
\sin^6 x \cos x + \cos^6 x &= (1 - \cos^2 x)(\sin^4 x + \\\sin^3 x \cos x + \sin^2 x \cos^2 x + \sin x \cos^3 + \cos^4 x).
\end{align*}Accordingly, we set
\begin{align*}
\sin^4 x + \sin^3 x \cos x + \sin^2 x \cos^2 x + \sin x \cos^3 + \cos^4 x &= \tan^2 x + 1,
\end{align*}so $(\tan \cdot (\tan^2 x + 1 - \tan^2 x)^2 = \tan^2 x (1 - \tan^4 x) + \tan^2 x = 2 \tan^2 x - \tan^4 x.$

Since $\sin x \cos x \le \frac{1}{2},$ $\sin^6 x \cos x + \cos^6 x \le \frac{1}{2},$ so $2 \tan^2 x - \tan^4 x \le 6.$  This simplifies to $\tan^4 x - 2 \tan^2 x - 6 \le 0.$  We have that the graph of $\tan^4 x - 2 \tan^2 x - 6$ crosses the $x$-axis at $\sqrt{2 + \sqrt{14}}$ and $-\sqrt{2 + \sqrt{14}}.$  Therefore, this quadratic is non-positive for $x \in$ $\boxed{\left[90^\circ, \arctan(\sqrt {2 + \sqrt {14}})\right] \cup \left[\arctan(- \sqrt {2 + \sqrt {14}}), 360^\circ\right]}.$ These are the first two cases.

Checking the second case by setting $\tan x = -\sqrt {2 + \sqrt {14}},$ we find that $\sin x = -\sqrt {\frac {14 + 2 \cdot \sqrt {14}}{20 + 4\cdot\sqrt{14}}
} = -\sqrt{\frac{7 + \sqrt{14}}{12 + 2\sqrt{14}}}, $ $\cos x = -\sqrt{\frac{7 - \sqrt{14}}{12 + 2\sqrt{14}}}$. This gives that $\cos^{-1} x = 180^{\circ} + \arcsin\left(2\sqrt{7 + \sqrt {14}}\right)$ which ends about $261$ degrees. Thus the range of $x$ is $[90^\circ,261^\circ] \cup [360^\circ, 309^\circ],$ and their sum is $351^\circ + 69^\circ = \boxed{420^\circ}.$ </think> <answer> 420^\circ </answer>

