 We first find the area of the larger square, which is $10^2=100$.  We then subtract the areas of the small squares and the triangle to find the area of the desired shaded region.  The less small square has area $3^2=9$, while the larger square has  area of $10^2=100$.  We now consider the triangle.  We extend the top side of the larger square to the right and complete the rectangle.  We have a right isosceles triangle with legs of 7, so it has an area of $\frac{7\cdot7}{2}=\frac{49}{2}$.  Thus, our answer is $100-9-\frac{49}{2}=\boxed{\frac{63}{2}}$. [asy]
